;;; `(absent ())` loads, and holds — M1f **W35**, register row
;;; `W35/absent-empty` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1f.5.0
;;; (2026-11-08) from S1f.5.5's census (§ Findings outside the grammar),
;;; filed `open` and owned by S1f.5.5, on the user's ruling of 2026-11-08 on
;;; the census (*"Q3 - as recommended"*).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two `p` facts and a rule whose `:match` writes `(absent ())` beside its
;;; premise `(p ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `()` is a whole `:match` or a whole `:assert` and nothing else (the paper
;;; grammar's default 7), so `(absent ())` is refused at load, with a line
;;; naming the rule and the clause: every command that reads the program
;;; exits 1 (`ein test`: 2). Read under F12, `()` is true, so its absence
;;; would never hold.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loads and `(absent ())` holds, so `rr` fires for every `p`:
;;; root derives `(hit a)` and `(hit b)`, H is empty and R is {∅}, exit 0
;;; (`ein saturate FILE --dump`, `ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at
;;; `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s, release and debug alike).

(relation p T) (relation hit T)
(p a) (p b)
(rule rr () :match (and (p ?x) (absent ())) :assert (hit ?x))
(query :goal (hit ?x))
